Chapter: RocTupleTwoLists
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Ported from roc-lang/roc, src/eval/test/eval_closure_recursion_tests.zig,
 "inspect: multiple lists in tuple", case 28 in file order:

     { x = [1, 2] y = [3, 4] t = (x, y) match t { (first, _) => match first { [a, b] => a + b, _ => 0 } } }

 and Roc expects 3.

 THE CONTROL FOR `roc-tuple-same-list-twice`, and Roc keeps both. Same tuple,
 same read of the same position, same answer; the one respect that differs is
 whether the two slots hold the SAME list or two different ones. A backend
 that shares when it should copy, or copies when it should share, moves one of
 the pair and not the other.

 Adaptations: `list-at` for the list pattern, `3.0` is `3`.

Section: Functions

  read-two : Integer -> Integer
  read-two (n) =
   let x = [1, 2]
   in let y = [3, 4]
   in let (first, second) = (x, y)
   in list-at first 0 + list-at first 1

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni (show (read-two 0))
  end
